#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d923a3-db7f-532b-a813-4f0f8279c875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326488} {:answer/id #uuid "5918cfd2-1259-5fc3-8344-c3492582a735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326489} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f6c20b-558c-5896-a131-57fa545f27c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326490} {:answer/id #uuid "75954da1-6d61-56d1-90af-589130ec7483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326491} {:answer/id #uuid "1b696a28-6a1d-5183-b89c-7be33107d7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75954da1-6d61-56d1-90af-589130ec7483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a136a672-4f55-5841-8df6-a4fe0d07dfcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "823545bb-0540-59df-9354-6b5f7bce2ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101506" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(1,5,-1)$, $Q(5,1,1)$, and $R(2,5,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨1,5,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨5,1,1⟩,\\,r=⟨2,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046481/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,5,0⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf1a3d83-c0d0-5d59-a725-a4e550616531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325340} {:answer/id #uuid "345b08c5-cf95-54c0-961f-48ebefe29864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325341} {:answer/id #uuid "15b71257-302d-58eb-b6d6-a575553f86d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325342} {:answer/id #uuid "61964971-6f18-575d-bb4c-ec4324e66d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325343} {:answer/id #uuid "f43dd61c-e951-51e1-b3f0-81494fd85fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345b08c5-cf95-54c0-961f-48ebefe29864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325341} :answer-field/id #uuid "885fe984-e9dc-55cb-800b-ef3684faddb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6924e182-afff-5ad5-8eac-9fcb60a7b5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-101515" :question/problem "Given that $p\\cdot q=11$, find the angle between $p=⟨t,t-1⟩$ and $q=⟨-4,-1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $p\\cdot q=11$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =11 \\\\ t\\cdot (-4)+(t-1)\\cdot (-1) & =11 \\\\ -4t-t+1 & =11 \\\\ -5t & =10 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨(-2),(-2)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $p$ and $q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,||\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{13}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}})≈{42.3}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a441dca-25da-57b4-b90c-27dfb3b87a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{105.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299813} {:answer/id #uuid "13964d5a-9967-5ef6-9b12-d693f54cae14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{123.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299814} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ae7195-cfbc-5e22-944d-afe431f5cdca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{100.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299815} {:answer/id #uuid "bda0f309-e6e5-5017-8ce0-b80ffba45cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{95.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299816} {:answer/id #uuid "86e52145-75b1-5720-bbb3-99b6a2e78b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{115.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a441dca-25da-57b4-b90c-27dfb3b87a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{105.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299813} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7c4f24-38ef-52a3-a79c-0c6c63b4ef63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47778997-e90f-5f7d-b2f9-574ccbc3b4d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125078" :question/problem "Given that $a\\cdot b=-9$, find the angle between $a=⟨-2t,3t⟩$ and $b=⟨3,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $a\\cdot b=-9$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-9 \\\\ -2t\\cdot 3+3t\\cdot 1 & =-9 \\\\ -6t+3t & =-9 \\\\ -3t & =-9 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨-2(3),\\,3(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{9}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{117}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt{13}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{130}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{130}})≈{105.3}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a48530c6-5151-5767-8d33-b9b385ac683b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46.18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325354} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b19f68-2650-526f-9f23-98b81778f5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{52.33}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325355} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a2d70f-606d-53d8-888c-2ef44dcc4886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37.75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325356} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f2d9ac-ab27-5537-88a9-ae6bb04c1126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325357} {:answer/id #uuid "5621eec5-b695-5579-abef-41be75308fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33.74}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f2d9ac-ab27-5537-88a9-ae6bb04c1126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325357} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffcb5a00-9fa0-5f57-8dd7-870c04f9bd64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30394f5f-9572-5a4f-bf32-50670ecbcc6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-19032" :question/problem "Given that $c\\cdot d=11$, find the angle between $c=⟨2t,1⟩$ and $d=⟨2,3⟩.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, since $c\\cdot d=11$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =11 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3 & =11 \\\\ 4t+3 & =11 \\\\ 4t & =8 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =⟨2(2),1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $c$ and $d$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,c\\,||\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}})≈{42.27}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16104c2f-285a-58e3-a912-1c5c32735793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269131} {:answer/id #uuid "d7796c45-683b-5992-8c9f-34c50eb36c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132} {:answer/id #uuid "551cfa18-cedc-5bbe-8306-3487d020644c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{112}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269133} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea035f0-6207-54f9-8514-fc252b0a6a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269134} {:answer/id #uuid "48b4dd83-fc3a-5279-9506-b1c1c610adae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7796c45-683b-5992-8c9f-34c50eb36c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132} :answer-field/id #uuid "0865b498-b4a5-5b7f-ba31-8063a63b2589" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ec9194-f492-518e-8975-e0a3b197c76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-19107" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,1,-1)$, $B(3,-2,2)$, and $C(-1,-2,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-2,2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046481/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}})≈{54}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
